  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>The spotted headstander, Chilodus punctatus, is a headstander from much of the Amazon, the western Orinoco and coastal rivers of Guyana and Suriname. Also called the 'checkered headstander', it lives in slow-moving rivers, oxbows and floodplain lakes, and its colour pattern appears to vary with locality. Shy and peaceful, it is sensitive to dissolved organic wastes and should never be introduced to a biologically-immature aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Described from 'Lake Amuku, upper Essequibo River system, Guyana', and currently considered to range throughout much of the Amazon system in Ecuador, Peru and Brazil plus the western Orinoco in Colombia, the Apeú drainage in Pará state, eastern Brazil and various coastal river systems of Guyana and Suriname.</p><p>Colour pattern appears to vary somewhat with locality.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Mostly inhabits slow-moving rivers, tributaries, oxbows and floodplain lakes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Requires an aquarium with minimum base dimensions of 90 × 30 cm or equivalent (about 81 litres).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>A biotope-style set-up could consist of a sandy substrate with driftwood roots and branches, leaf litter and floating vegetation.</p><p>Submerged vegetation can also be used and although it may browse algae and suchlike from the surface this species should not consume leaves or soft shoots.</p><p>It's sensitive to dissolved organic wastes and should never be introduced to a biologically-immature aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–28°C</li><li>pH: 5.0–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 1–10 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Omnivorous and will accept most foods offered, with the stomach contents of wild specimens varying on a seasonal basis but mostly comprising insects and insect larvae (orders Trichoptera, Hemiptera, Homoptera) plus some terrestrial fruits and even scales of other fishes (Sánchez et al., 2003).</p><p>In the aquarium offer live Daphnia, Artemia, Moina, bloodworm, etc., vegetable matter in the form of blanched spinach, lettuce, cucumber, fruit, algae wafers, etc., and good quality, sinking dried foods.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Relatively shy and peaceful and does not appreciate the presence of boisterous or fast-moving species.</p><p>Good tankmates include many tetras, smaller loricariids, Corydoras spp. and peaceful cichlids such as Cleithracara or Mikrogeophagus spp.</p><p>It can be maintained in a group; although some squabbling is inevitable physical damage is rare provided sufficient cover is available.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Adult males develop a slightly more-extended dorsal fin than females, while mature females tend to have an overall rounder shape than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>Has apparently occurred although details have not been obtained thus far.</p><p>Presumably it's an egg scatterer exhibiting no parental care that spawns at the onset of the wet season.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<p>This species is sometimes referred to as 'checkered headstander'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/chilodus-punctatus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Reis, R. E., S. O. Kullander and C. J. Ferraris, Jr. (eds) (2003). Check list of the freshwater fishes of South and Central America. CLOFFSCA. EDIPUCRS, Porto Alegre, i-xi + 1-729</li><li>Sánchez, R. M., G. Galvis and P. F. Victoriano (2003). Relationship between digestive tract characteristics and diets of fishes from Yucao River, Meta River system (Colombia). Gayana (Concepción) 67(1), 75-86</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
